        "title": "Rope and Cordage - Landscape Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Rope is a composite structure: fibers twisted or braided into yarns, yarns into strands, strands into rope. Each level of the hierarchy trades some tensile efficiency for structural stability, handling properties, and resistance to failure by kinking, bending, or untwisting. The central design tension is between strength (maximized by straight parallel fibers) and usefulness (maximized by the helical twist that gives the rope its ability to be coiled, knotted, and handled)."
    }
